const composedRefs = useComposedRefs(forwardedRef, slottableElementRef);

if (!slottableElement) { ... return children; }

const mergedProps = mergeProps(slotProps, slottableElement.props ?? {});

// do not pass ref to React.Fragment for React 19 compatibility
if (slottableElement.type !== React.Fragment) {
  mergedProps.ref = forwardedRef ? composedRefs : slottableElementRef;
}

return React.cloneElement(slottableElement, mergedProps);
```

When `forwardedRef` is undefined: `mergedProps.ref = slottableElementRef` — same as before my change (old code: `mergedProps.ref = forwardedRef ? composedRefs : slottableElementRef;`). So the ref passed to the cloned element is identical: the child's own ref. No composition happens.

But wait — there's a subtle point about the old code: `composedRefs` was computed (via `composeRefs`) even when `forwardedRef` was undefined, but it wasn't used. The new code calls `useComposedRefs` unconditionally — this hook runs `React.useCallback(...)` — but has no observable side effect on behavior.

One subtle behavior: when `forwardedRef` is undefined and `slottableElementRef` is the child's ref callback... the identity of the ref passed to the cloned element is `slottableElementRef` itself (a stable element ref). Same as before.

So behavior when no forwardedRef: the cloned element receives exactly the child's original ref — identical to before the fix.

